// @run
// @log 3
// @log true
// @log 5
// @log false
// TWO inline shapes that share a field NAME and a wasm CODE but not a TYPE TEXT
// (`{b: i32}` and `{b: boolean}` — both code 0, i32 storage), reached as nested fields of
// union variants. Both the NAMED-variant spelling and the INLINE-ARM spelling are here, and
// before this repair BOTH reported `emitProgram: nested-struct field element type is not
// interned` — the arm spelling has floored this way for as long as it has had the code-15
// deferral, so this is a pre-existing defect, not a new one.
//
// THE AXIS IS CODE EQUALITY WITHOUT TEXT EQUALITY, AT THE SAME FIELD NAME. Measured cell by
// cell, each its own program:
//   - `{b: i32}` beside `{b: boolean}` — floored (one code, two texts);
//   - `{b: i32}` beside `{c: i32}` — different field NAME, no dedup, always worked;
//   - `{b: i32}` beside `{b: i32}` — identical, dedups onto itself, always worked;
//   - `{b: i32}` beside `{b: string}` — different CODE, no dedup, always worked.
// Storage class, nesting depth, arm count and the OUTER field's name are all free: the
// collision is entirely between the two INNER shapes.
//
// THE MECHANISM: `annShapeIndexOf` treats one wasm code as one layout, deliberately, so
// `{b: boolean}` resolves to the existing `{b: i32}` row (field name equal, code 0 equal, no
// element name to part on) and no row spelled `{b: boolean}` is ever minted. The variant
// field's one code-15 consumer looks its element up BY NAME (`structIndexByName`), so the
// recorded spelling read -1 for a shape whose LAYOUT was interned, under its twin's name.
// `collectA`'s deferred code-15 intern now re-keys the row to `sNames[<interned index>]` —
// the discipline `internInlineShapeTy`'s own nested-field arm already applied to its field
// texts, applied to the one recorder that discarded the index. Storage-correct by
// construction: the two spellings dedup precisely because they store identically, which is
// also why the re-key is inert wherever the names already agreed — 1,522 of 1,522
// pre-existing `@run` corpus modules stay byte-identical.
//
// THE ORDER DEPENDENCE IS PART OF WHAT THIS PINS. The corpus oracle drives many programs
// through ONE compiler instance, and the floor reproduced ACROSS that boundary as well as
// within a single program — a `{b: i32}` shape in one case file and a `{b: boolean}` shape in
// the next reddened the second. A fixture that only held the single-program form would go
// green on a repair that left the cross-program one standing.
type P1 = { x: { b: i32 } }
type UP = P1 | i32

type P2 = { y: { b: boolean } }
type UQ = P2 | i32

const p: UP = { x: { b: 3 } }
if p is P1 { print(p.x.b) } else { print(0) }

const q: UQ = { y: { b: true } }
if q is P2 { print(q.y.b) } else { print(0) }

// The same collision in the INLINE-ARM spelling, whose recorder has always had the
// deferral and so has always had the floor.
type UR = { z: { d: i32 } } | i32
type US = { e: { d: boolean } } | i32

const r: UR = { z: { d: 5 } }
if r is { z: { d: i32 } } { print(r.z.d) } else { print(0) }

const s: US = { e: { d: false } }
if s is { e: { d: boolean } } { print(s.e.d) } else { print(0) }
